i'm the king of sketch design what do  you think that paper crown makes you the  king of sketch design  the crown doesn't make me king the  checklist  on the back of the crown makes me king  what's going on guys my name is david  thomach and welcome back to the channel  probably don't need the crown on for  this video let's put that away  today we're talking about sketch design  and what you need to do  to be able to produce a good set of  sketch design documents  i know a few of you been asking so take  some notes and let's get started so  there are a number of things that you  need to follow  in a sketch design set of documents  however there's only really  three main sheets that you need to  produce the cover page is obvious i'm  not even going to talk about the cover  page  but you need to include one a site plan  to the floor plan or floor plans  depending on how many levels it is  and three elevations the purpose of the  site plan at sketch design stage is to  indicate  the location of the design against the  property  it is very important that you get some  sort of feature survey  that will be able to tell you the levels  and the contours of that block  because that is absolutely crucial if  you go and design for a flat block  and then all of a sudden you find out  that block is sloping  five meters every single meter you're in  big trouble  so the first key element is obviously  get a feature survey a couple basic  points to get out the way  is you need a north point and a scale  bar on the site plan  you also need your title block with all  the details filled in  when we look at the actual drawing you  need to showcase the feature survey  which shows the boundaries and all the  levels on it you need to showcase the  external walls  only of the property we don't need to  see any internals just yet  so external walls only you want to be  able to showcase all the plumbing  features as well  that means sinks toilets showers baths  etc whatever you can think of that's  plumbing related  make sure you show it on the site plan  stairs and ramps are also an important  factor  that you show on sketch design for the  site plan it's something that's  transitioned all the way through towards  the end  but it's good practice to keep at the  very beginning the rest of the site then  indicates the landscaping the driveway  the pathways  and anything that might be of critical  importance if you have a very large tree  in the middle  it might be good to note that you might  need to remove that tree or design  around it  finally the one thing that you want to  showcase is your area calculations how  big  is the ground floor living how big is  the garage how big is the storeroom  how big is the alfresco these are the  things that people need to know  especially if you're going to a build up  very early on they can quickly look at  these numbers  put some of their own numbers together  and spit out a very generic rough price  now if you aren't taking notes that's  okay this checklist is available to  download on my website  so if you look down in the description  below you'll find a link to this  checklist to download  today and because it is a 28 day  challenge i will be giving away  10 of these checklists to you guys to be  a winner all you have to do is subscribe  to this channel and  follow me on instagram leave your  instagram handle in the comments below  and i will pick a winner at the end of  february the site plan in general  should be at a minimum of 1 to 200 scale  you want it to be this size because it  doesn't need to showcase  detailed information but it needs to  showcase the whole site  moving on to the site plan the site plan  gets a little bit bigger  it goes to one to 100 scale because you  want to showcase  everything inside that building  depending on the size of the design  if one to 100 doesn't fit on a3 you're  going to have to move up to a2  a1 or a0 once you hit the a0 stage if it  still doesn't fit at 1 to 100  then it's up to you to decide if you  want to shrink it down  or if you want to split it across  multiple pages similar to the site plan  you want to showcase your north point  your title bar and of course your scale  bar  on the floor plan itself we continue to  show just the boundary lines of the  property  not the rest of the feature survey  information we also show  all the external walls and the plumbing  the stairs everything we showcased on  the site plan  but also start to include the more  detailed items like the internal walls  all the internal doors  all the openings all the doors all the  windows the floor plan is about showing  size scale and moveability so it's  important that you start showcasing  some numbers straight away you want to  be able to understand how big a room is  and what that room is  so make sure you title it for example  bedroom one and underneath  you have the size of that room for  example four meters by four meters  something to take note of is furniture  isn't critical but furniture is a good  indicator for clients who don't know  about scale drawings  to be able to understand space if you  put a bed  inside a bedroom and you can see a  decent amount of space next to it  the client's immediately going to know  that yep okay i have plenty of space in  that room  alternatively if you show that bed in a  room that's too small  then obviously the client is going to be  able to pick up and go hmm that room  seems a little bit too small for me i'd  like to be able to add a chair for  example  so again furniture not critical but it's  definitely something that adds to the  experience you also want to showcase all  four elevation markers of the entire  property so if there's four sides you're  obviously going to show four  if there's more than four sides and you  need to showcase more well then it's up  to you and your discretion  to make more elevation markers if need  be if you're designing a two story three  story or more  it's also important to showcase the roof  on the floor below  now finally the elevations are again one  to one hundred  and they are a lot more diagrammatic at  this point in time  the purpose of the elevations is to  showcase the architectural  interpretation and expression  it's to understand what the architect is  thinking and to convey that to the  client  so it is predominantly about materiality  and scale like all your other pages the  title block remains on that page  however the north point and the scale  bar can be removed  because now you're showing four  elevations more than likely on one page  the orientation of the north point is  very different so it's no longer  required  it's important that we start showing a  little bit more detail in relation to  the site here  so we want to be able to showcase the  natural ground level which is  interpreted  from the feature survey information and  the future ground level  if you are altering that site in any way  shape or form so for example if it is a  cliff and you want to bank into it build  a retaining wall  it's important that you show one that  retaining wall and two  that new ground level that is eventually  going to form that level of the house  we predominantly focus here on the  external walls the windows the doors and  the roof  you want to be able to showcase all of  these elements but at the same time  show a materiality legend or some sort  of key to indicate  what each material is some people do it  by actually noting each individual item  at sketch design other people  note it with a legend by cropping out a  tiny piece of the building  moving it across and saying this is  glass window for example  how you do it is completely up to you  but one thing that you do have to  remember  is again that scale proportion so we  want to indicate  story heights and story levels so if you  start at the ground floor you move up  for example to the ceiling of the ground  floor which is  two seven it can be 2.7 meters above  natural ground level and then you might  have your next floor which is three one  and then your next floor which is  another two seven above it so it'd be  plus five eight  i think you get my point if you don't  i'm happy to explain further in the  comments down below just let me know  and that's all you really need to know  for sketch design guys it's three very  basic pages with some basic information  that gets the architectural idea across  the line anyway that's all for me today  guys thanks so much for watching i hope  you enjoyed the video  if you did make sure you smash that  subscribe button down below  and because it is february i will see  you tomorrow
 

